{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793908651,"nonce":"y60V1YoBcuNdtUuvSHKHYm6pXZk=","message":"Initial iOS accessibility implementation and current-source Simulator validation completed locally, 2026-11-05. This supersedes the prior baseline with no native control descendants. Changes are uncommitted; the issue remains open.\n\nAdded a shared semantic snapshot and assistive actions in blinc_layout: stable IDs, names/roles/values/states, clipped visible bounds, hidden subtrees, active overlay filtering, explicit label references and reactive input names. Actions reuse control handlers and text-input document/change paths, reject removed/disabled controls, and support UTF-16 edit ranges with masked secure values. CN inputs forward their label and Return callback; checkbox rows now expose their semantic type and existing row handlers.\n\niOS publishes the snapshot through a C ABI and a retained UIKit accessibility container. Stable IDs retain native object identity. Native single-line text-field proxies provide editing while GPU rendering remains the visual source; focus, selection, Return submission, keyboard bridge and insets remain synchronized. UIKit hosts use a common Dynamic Type scale for content, control frames and input coordinates. Updated the current host, platform template and CLI-generated host; the CLI bundles its own template for publication. Generated-host typechecking also removed a macOS-only CAMetalLayer property from the iOS template. Existing iOS frame construction now uses the complete UI/style/registry build path.\n\nFinal dedicated iPhone SE3/arm64/iOS17.5 Simulator result: 4 tests passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, 0 expected failures. Three UI tests cover exposed semantics/actions/editing/Return, actual rendered larger-text geometry and the unfiltered Apple accessibility audit; one hosted native test invokes accessibility activation and editing delegates directly and checks stable objects. Reviewed normal/larger-text GPU captures and the final edited screenshot (Ada, Count:11, checked checkbox). The general audit is accompanied by explicit semantic assertions, not used alone as acceptance.\n\nEvidence: target/ios-accessibility/results-1793908304575159000.xcresult and matching -summary.json/-attachments/manifest.json. Edited capture: -attachments/BC687CCD-6616-431E-B7C9-08D92B8B92D1.png. Console /private/tmp/blinc-accessibility-ios-verified.log. memory.json records outcome=passed and shutdown_returncode=0; a final simctl booted-device inventory is empty. No Android/QEMU or Simulator GUI started. Cargo jobs2, Xcode jobs1; telemetry only, no memory guard.\n\nScoped validation: 8 semantic-tree/action tests, 1 CLI-generated Swift host/typecheck test, 2 passive-telemetry runner tests, mdBook, 18 touched Rust-file format checks, whitespace checks and bundled/canonical Swift-template parity pass. All 15 cn_keyboard_focus and 16 keyboard_nav cases return success in individual processes. The combined cn_keyboard_focus suite still has the same 8 passes/7 failures on both unchanged HEAD4b30bd76 and this patch; details remain open on keyboard tracker017446dd. Existing headless GPU guards remain, so process-isolated status is not a new no-skip guarantee.\n\nValidation correction: the baseline comparison reused the root Cargo target directory and contaminated provisional artifacts. Explicitly invalidated compiled caches only for blinc_layout, blinc_cn, blinc_app and blinc_recorder, then rebuilt current sources and reran the final 8 semantic tests, generated-host check and all31 isolated keyboard cases. Final /private/tmp/blinc-accessibility-keyboard-isolated/build.jsonl records fresh=false artifacts from the current root manifests. iOS uses a separate cache and was unaffected. Final evidence supersedes provisional isolated-case results.\n\nSupported coverage is deliberately bounded: buttons, labelled single-line input, checkbox/disabled state, native activation and larger-text layout on the fixture. Still open: physical-device VoiceOver, assistive scrolling/offscreen traversal, semantic providers for other editable widgets, advanced composite semantics and screen-reader focus restoration across overlays. Browser, Android and desktop adapters remain unimplemented. Public accessibility guide and changelogs document these limits. No full-library accessibility or overall release-readiness claim; no issue closure, commit, push or publication.","files":null}]}